    "story": "Old bridge. Still holds. Timber groans the same way it always has. River’s up — I can smell it. Cold on the steel, soft in the stack. Wind’s crosswise, smoke’s pulling crooked. No one speaks out here. That’s fine. I don’t need to be heard to be felt.",
    "notes": "The vertical framing emphasizes scale — both upward and downward — creating tension between the engine’s mass and the sky it disturbs. The bridge introduces narrative pause: a moment of weight suspended, motion made vulnerable. Tonally, the image blends the realism of early industrial photography with moody saturation and depth — a subtle nod to pictorialism, especially in the painterly treatment of the treetops and sky. Like much of Heim’s work, it rewards stillness: the longer you look, the more you feel it moving.",
